     1	# Tessera — Phase 0 Pre-Registration, Amendment 10 — six wording corrections to Amendment 9, found by the plain-language pilot (2026-10-16)
     2	
     3	> **Status: PROPOSED. This document is the instrument and is not yet signed. The author's first commit containing
     4	> this file is its signing act** (the house form since Amendment 5). Drafted 2026-10-16 by the AI collaborator
     5	> (Claude, Opus 5.5, owner instance) from `docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md` ("Findings about A9 itself",
     6	> A01–A06), A9, A8 §A8.3 and §A8.5, A2 §A2.1, A1 §A1.6, `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §7.1–§7.2 and
     7	> `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` §7. Layered on the original and Amendments 1–9. Per amend-don't-rewrite, none of them
     8	> is edited: every registered sentence this amendment changes is listed in §A10.7 with its source line and its
     9	> replacement, and is left standing in its file. **A10.4 is AWAITING AUTHOR RULING.** The author rules on it in his
    10	> own words before the signing commit; if he rejects or changes it, the owner revises A10.4 first.
    11	
    12	## In plain words
    13	
    14	This amendment corrects the wording of six places in Amendment 9. It adds no mechanism. It changes no check,
    15	verdict, parameter or test vector. A review of the plain version of Amendment 9 found the six places.
    16	
    17	- **A01.** Amendment 9 says that the content existed "no later than" T. T is a chain time, not real time. The
    18	  correction says this in the claim.
    19	- **A02.** Amendment 9 says that the keys can issue receipts "inside the window". The verifier makes only two
    20	  comparisons with the window. The correction states only those two comparisons.
    21	- **A03.** Amendment 9 excludes Bitcoin history "deeper than k". The correction defines the depth of a
    22	  reorganization. The exclusion then includes a reorganization of exactly k blocks, as the models do.
    23	- **A04.** Amendment 9 does not say which receipts V1 protects after an attacker obtains authority-record
    24	  credentials. The recommended text protects the receipts from before the capture. It does not prevent new
    25	  receipts after the capture. The public git repository makes those new receipts visible. The author must decide
    26	  this item.
    27	- **A05.** Amendment 9 does not give the order of the steps that select a Bitcoin attestation. The correction
    28	  gives the steps that the profile already uses.
    29	- **A06.** Amendment 9 says "owner name D". The correct DNS owner name is `_tessera-v1.wamason.com`. The correction
    30	  names it.
    31	
    32	Each correction has a kind:
    33	
    34	- CLARIFY: the meaning does not change.
    35	- NARROW-CLAIM: the claim becomes smaller, so that it states only what the checks establish.
    36	- CORRECT: the wording disagreed with a registered rule or model, and the correction follows that rule or model.
    37	
    38	---
    39	
    40	**Provenance labels** (as in A9). **AUTHOR DECISION**: the author ruled, in his own quoted words, dated. **OWNER
    41	DECISION**: taken by the owner instance under the project ownership the author assigned on 2026-10-12; not an
    42	author ruling. **RECOMMENDED**: drafted, not decided by anyone. What the signature means for OWNER items is as in
    43	A9: the author has read them and does not object.
    44	
    45	**Weakening (A1 §A1.1).** No A1.2 property, registered check, verdict, reason code, parameter (δ, ε, k, N, S),
    46	vector, encoding or model changes. A10.1, A10.2 and A10.4 narrow sentences of A9's prose claim to what A9's own
    47	checks, A8.3's runs and the cited models establish; no evidence is withdrawn. A10.3 removes one boundary case from
    48	the claim that the registered assumption (A1.6) and the model (P5c) never covered. This signed amendment is the
    49	record A1 §A1.1 requires for each narrowing.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
    54	
    55	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :133 and in
    56	C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff "requires an
    57	additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary reading of "no
    58	later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
    59	
    60	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
    61	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
    62	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
    63	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
    64	backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish. The changed sentences are rows 1–4 of A10.7.
    65	
    66	## A10.2 The window establishes two comparisons, not issuance "inside" it — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A02)
    67	
    68	A9's opening says the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window" (:36), and guarantee 2's heading says
    69	"Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time" (:441). The C4 verifier rule (:419-421) checks
    70	only two comparisons, and says "T itself is not compared with not-before".
    71	
    72	**Reading adopted.** The verifier establishes exactly two inclusive comparisons, with no margin:
    73	- the declared issue time is not earlier than `issuance-not-before`;
    74	- the verified chain-time T is not later than `issuance-not-after`.
    75	
