   117	## Findings for non-FAITHFUL sentences
   118	
   119	### F01
   120	
   121	**S07 — DROPS the condition on the four checks.** Pilot: “A receipt proves four things:”. A9 32–34: “Provided the service's signing capability was not misused within the key's window, a V1 receipt lets anyone, with no network and no help from Tessera, check four things”. The pilot's later statement about cryptography does not restore the condition on misuse. “Proves” also conceals the distinction between a conditional claim and an unconditional fact.
   122	
   123	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   124	
   125	```text
   126	A receipt supports these four checks under both conditions:
   127	- The registered cryptographic and key-use assumptions hold.
   128	- Nobody misused the service's signing capability within the window.
   129	```
   130	
   131	### F02
   132	
   133	**S10 and S51 — DROPS the evidence-time qualification.** Pilot: “Two authority records said that these keys belong to wamason.com, for a stated window.” / “Two authority records said that the keys belong to wamason.com, for the window.” C4: “authenticated K for D, with the issuance window, at their evidence times.” These are historical assertions evaluated at particular evidence times, not a claim of continuing authority. The later descriptions never restore those evaluation times.
   134	
   135	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   136	
   137	```text
   138	Both authority records say the two keys belong to wamason.com.
   139	They give the window in which those keys can issue receipts.
   140	The verifier must check each authority record at that record's evidence time.
   141	```
   142	
   143	F13 and F14 specify the different checks for the two records.
   144	
   145	### F03
   146	
   147	**S15 — CHANGES who checks the window.** Pilot: “The receipt also checks the window.” A9 419–421: “Verifier rule (C4). A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless” the two stated comparisons hold. A receipt supplies evidence; the verifier performs the check.
   148	
   149	Proposed plain text:
   150	
   151	```text
   152	The verifier also checks the window.
   153	```
   154	
   155	Keep S16 and S17 as a bulleted list after that sentence. Their two inclusive comparisons are faithful. Do not replace them with “both times must be inside the window”: A9 does not require that.
   156	
   157	### F04
   158	
   159	**S21 — DROPS ordinary replay and reattribution.** Pilot: “The attacker uses a true receipt again for different content, or puts a false date on it.” A9 40: “replays, reattributes or backdates receipts”. Reuse with different content describes one substitution attempt. It does not cover another presentation of the same receipt and content, or a false claim about which keys or service made it. C2 explicitly leaves transaction suitability unresolved.
   160	
   161	Proposed plain text **[D]** (three bullets in the attack list):
   162	
   163	```text
   164	- The attacker presents a receipt again.
   165	- The attacker claims that different keys or a different service made the receipt.
   166	- The attacker claims an earlier issue time for a receipt.
   167	```
   168	
   169	These protections do not promise transaction-specific replay prevention. Keep C2's transaction non-claim beside them.
   170	
   171	### F05
   172	
   173	**S23 — DROPS later signing capability without key theft.** Pilot: “The attacker steals a key after Bitcoin anchored the receipt.” A9 42–43: “steals a key, gains signing capability, or breaks a signature algorithm after a receipt was anchored.” S24 covers the algorithm case. A non-exportable key can remain secret while an attacker gains permission to use it (C3; A9 387–388).
   174	
   175	Proposed additional bullet **[D]**:
   176	
   177	```text
   178	- The attacker gains signing capability after Bitcoin anchors the receipt.
   179	```
   180	
   181	Keep the theft and algorithm bullets. Do not equate signing capability with possession of private key bytes.
   182	
   183	### F06
   184	
   185	**S25 — DROPS the registered nature and specificity of the assumptions.** Pilot: “These protections are true only if the cryptography, the key rules and Bitcoin work as expected.” A9 43: “under the registered cryptographic, key-use and chain assumptions.” “Work as expected” substitutes an unspecified expectation for a registered condition. It can also imply that correct cryptographic calculations alone establish historical key safety, which C7 explicitly does not evaluate.
   186	
   187	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   188	
   189	```text
   190	These protections depend on the registered cryptographic, key-use and Bitcoin history assumptions.
   191	```
   192	
   193	Put exact source references at the end. The reviewer cannot replace those assumptions with a general assurance.
   194	
   195	### F07
   196	
   197	**S34 — CHANGES whole operational capture into simultaneous account capture.** Pilot: “An attacker who controls all of the author's accounts at the same time.” A9 49: “whole-author-sphere capture (R-2.15)”. The referenced R-2.15 says “the author's entire operational sphere at issue time”; A1 452–458 makes the same distinction. The sphere includes operational control beyond accounts, and the source names issue time, not any simultaneous compromise at any time.
   198	
   199	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   200	
   201	```text
   202	V1 does not protect against capture of the author's whole operational sphere at issue time.
   203	This includes the accounts, devices and signing capability that the author uses for the service.
   204	```
   205	
   206	The vocabulary needs an explicit definition of “operational sphere”; “all accounts” is not that definition.
   207	
   208	### F08
   209	
   210	**S35 — DROPS the git exclusions and obscures the Bitcoin-history exclusion.** Pilot: “An attacker who breaks SHA-256, OpenTimestamps or more than six Bitcoin blocks.” A9 49–51: “breaks of SHA-256, OpenTimestamps, Bitcoin history deeper than k, or, in git's links, a second-preimage break of SHA-1 or attacker influence over committed contents”. Individual blocks are not the object of the stated history-depth limit. Both git exclusions are entirely absent.
   211	
   212	Proposed bullets **[D, A]**:
   213	
   214	```text
   215	- The attacker breaks SHA-256.
   216	- The attacker breaks OpenTimestamps.
   217	- The attacker changes Bitcoin history deeper than k, where k is six.
   218	- The attacker finds different git bytes with the same SHA-1 hash as given git bytes.
   219	- The attacker influences the contents of a git commit.
   220	```
   221	
   222	The SHA-1 sentence describes a second-preimage attack, not merely finding any two colliding objects. Explain the technical names at first use. A03 below identifies the unresolved history-depth counting boundary; the proposed text retains A9's threshold rather than choosing another one.
   223	
   224	### F09
   225	
   226	**S40 — DROPS common metadata, slot-specific key binding, and the frame distinction.** Pilot: “Both keys signed the same content.” C1: “both bind the same content and common metadata, the designated fingerprint in its slot”; non-claim: “Payload identity is not frame equality.” Equal content alone is a weaker condition. The signed frames contain additional information and need not be identical.
   227	
   228	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   229	
   230	```text
   231	Each key signs its own frame.
   232	Both frames must bind the same content and common metadata.
   233	Each frame must name the key fingerprint for its assigned slot.
   234	The frames can differ even when their content matches.
   235	```
   236	
   237	Define frame, common metadata, key fingerprint and slot before this use. The last sentence restores C1's otherwise missing non-claim.
   238	
   239	### F10
   240	
   241	**S41 — CHANGES an unambiguous encoding into a claim about a receipt ID.** Pilot: “The receipt ID has only one possible meaning.” C1: “The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` over both frames and both signatures is unambiguous.” The dictionary defines receipt ID as a SHA-256 hash of an undefined “signed core” (Style 34). That is a different object and property. Unique parsing of `A_sig` does not assert unconditional uniqueness of every possible hash preimage, nor the meaning or truth of content.
   242	
   243	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   244	
   245	```text
   246	A_sig holds both frames and their exact signatures in a fixed format.
   247	The verifier reads A_sig in only one way.
   248	```
   249	
   250	Here `A_sig` is an unchanged technical identifier, explained by the first sentence. Repair the dictionary separately if receipt ID remains in a future pilot. Do not describe `A_sig` as a frame or a hash.
   251	
   252	### F11
   253	
   254	**S43 — DROPS “at some issuance event,” not the existential quantifier.** Pilot: “The service received the content at some time.” C2: “The content was presented at some issuance event of the V1 service (existential).” “At some time” correctly preserves “there was at least one”; it does not assert a unique event or an earliest time. But ordinary receipt of content by the service, for example before an unsuccessful attempt, is broader than presentation at an issuance event.
   255	
   256	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   257	
   258	```text
   259	The service received this content during at least one issuance event.
   260	```
   261	
   262	Do not add “this receipt's only issuance event” or “the person holding it submitted it.” Neither follows from C2.
   263	
   264	### F12
   265	
   266	**S48 — DROPS independent fates and narrows independent approval to two people.** Pilot: “Not claimed: that two people must agree before the service signs.” C3: “V1 claims no separation of control and no independent fates, and no independent approval of content.” S37–S38 and S49 restore shared control and separate technical systems. They do not restore independent-fate or content-approval non-claims. Independent approval is not simply a count of people.
   267	
   268	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   269	
   270	```text
   271	One person controls both keys.
   272	The keys use separate technical systems.
   273	V1 does not promise independent control or independent approval of content.
   274	V1 does not promise that the keys fail independently.
   275	```
   276	
   277	Also restore the non-exportability distinction as O02. Separate technical systems do not establish independent availability or independent signing fates (A9 393–394).
   278	
   279	### F13
   280	
   281	**S52 — CHANGES DNSSEC validation into DNSSEC doing the signing, and DROPS its evaluation time.** Pilot: “One record is in DNS, which DNSSEC signs.” C4: “DNSSEC (owner name D, evaluated at the snapshot's own anchored time)”. DNSSEC is the mechanism used to authenticate the DNS evidence; the pilot makes the protocol the signer of DNS itself. It also omits the required owner-name check and the snapshot's independent evaluation time.
   282	
   283	Proposed plain text **[D, A]**:
   284	
   285	```text
   286	The verifier checks the DNS authority record with DNSSEC.
   287	It must check the required DNS owner name for wamason.com.
   288	It must check DNSSEC at the DNS snapshot's own anchor time.
   289	```
   290	
   291	Explain DNSSEC first. Do not substitute the receipt's T for the snapshot's time. A06 addresses the source's shorthand “owner name D.”
   292	
   293	### F14
   294	
   295	**S53 — CHANGES the signed object and DROPS full-path verification.** Pilot: “The other record is in a git repository, which the repository key signs.” C4: “the anchored git key assertion (full commit→tree→blob path)”. The opening identifies “a signed, timestamped git commit” (A9 35). A9 297–299 specifies “the signed commit's object id” and verification of `gpgsig` and the path. Signing an entire repository is not the stated mechanism; merely signing some record is insufficient.
   296	
   297	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   298	
   299	```text
   300	The repository key signs a git commit.
   301	The verifier must check that signature.
   302	It must check the complete commit-to-tree-to-blob path to the authority record.
   303	OpenTimestamps anchors the signed commit's object ID in Bitcoin.
   304	```
   305	
   306	Define repository key, commit, tree, blob and object ID. The dictionary's service-only definition of “key” currently cannot cover the repository key without qualification.
   307	
   308	### F15
   309	
   310	**S54 — DROPS selection of the two anchors used for the ordering test.** Pilot: “Bitcoin anchored the git record at least six blocks before it anchored the receipt.” C4: “the git assertion's selected anchor height plus 5 is lower than the receipt's selected anchor height”. A comparison between any convenient pair of anchors is not the registered comparison. The omitted “selected” matters when a proof contains multiple attestations.
   311	
   312	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   313	
   314	```text
   315	The verifier selects a Bitcoin block for the git record and another for the receipt.
   316	The selected git block number plus five must be lower than the selected receipt block number.
   317	```
   318	
   319	There is **no arithmetic off-by-one error** in the pilot's “at least six blocks before” by itself: integer heights satisfying `g + 5 < r` have `r − g ≥ 6`. The sixth confirmation of the git anchor is at `g + 5`; the receipt anchor must follow it. This is distinct from source issue A03 about reorganization depth.
   320	
   321	### F16
   322	
   323	**S60 — DROPS verified proof scope and presentation-sensitive selection.** Pilot: “T comes from the Bitcoin attestation with the lowest block number in the receipt.” C5: “the verified attestation at the lowest block height in the presented OTS proof (both T and its confirmations come from that attestation”; its non-claim is “Presentation-sensitive T.” A low number merely appearing in a receipt is not verified evidence. The same selected proof path supplies T and confirmations, and a different presented proof can give a different T for the same signed receipt.
   324	
   325	Proposed plain text **[D, A]**:
   326	
   327	```text
   328	The verifier selects the verified Bitcoin block with the lowest number in the presented OpenTimestamps proof.
   329	T comes from that block.
   330	The verifier takes the confirmation count from that same proof path.
   331	Different proofs for one receipt can give different values of T.
   332	```
   333	
   334	A05 below identifies a source ambiguity about selecting evidence that is not covered by pins; this replacement does not silently resolve it.
   335	
   336	### F17
