   406	**S71 — CHANGES a bounded, unestablished acceptance criterion into a general rule about bad receipts.** Pilot: “A person who is not an expert must not accept a bad receipt as good.” C7: “a non-expert makes no unsafe accept on the mapping (4 Dec criterion, not yet established).” A9 118–122 limits the evidence to one independent human tester and expressly says it is “not as a result about relying parties in general.” An unsafe accept also includes relying on a valid receipt for something it does not establish: e/e′ need non-revocation, f does not establish submitter identity, and i′ can have a valid Tessera receipt with an invalid party signature (A9 183–187). “Bad receipt” loses these distinctions. S73 preserves the fact that testing is unfinished, but does not repair the scope of the criterion.
