   526	
   527	## Findings about A9 itself for the next amendment
   528	
   529	These are source findings exposed by the rendering, separate from translation defects and dictionary defects. Some are ambiguities requiring clarification, not demonstrated failures of the implementation or formal models. The pilot must not settle them silently. References outside the requested paragraphs identify rules A9 itself invokes; they do not substitute another draft for A9.
   530	
   531	### A01 — “existed no later than T” needs an explicit clock meaning
   532	
   533	A9 36–37: “the bytes and both signatures existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin.” C5 repeats “existed no later than T” and also says “Header times are chain-time proxies.” A9 444–446 says a finite UTC cutoff needs an additional backward-skew assumption and “A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C”.
   534	
   535	Those sentences do not support the ordinary-language interpretation that the bytes existed before the real-world instant denoted by T. An inclusion block can carry a backward-skewed header time. The preimage must exist before inclusion; it need not exist before that header's nominal UTC reading. Merely saying “not exact” or “close” does not solve this.
   536	
   537	**Next amendment finding:** explicitly distinguish existence before inclusion in the selected block from an upper bound in real-world time. State which meaning the claim adopts. If it adopts a UTC bound, register the additional assumption and evidence; otherwise qualify the opening and C5. This is a source-level tension, not only the pilot's unsupported closeness assertion. S11 and S59 reproduce the source faithfully but inherit it.
   538	
   539	### A02 — “inside the window” overstates the actual two comparisons
   540	
   541	A9 441: “Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.” A9 419–421 specifies only declared issue time ≥ not-before and T ≤ not-after, and explicitly says “T itself is not compared with not-before.” A9's opening and A9.1 repeat those asymmetric comparisons. A9 407 calls the values an “Issuance window”.
   542	
   543	These are not a rule that both time values, or the actual act of signing, lie inside the interval. Two numerical examples expose the distinction under A2's preserved tolerances:
   544	
   545	| Example | Declared issue time | T | Designated sixth-block time | Why the stated comparisons permit it |
   546	|---|---|---|---|---|
   547	| Before-start T | window start | start minus 1 hour | start plus 1 hour | T ≥ declared minus 24 hours; all upper time bounds hold. T need not reach the window start. |
   548	| After-end declared time | window end plus 1 hour | window end | end plus 1 hour | Declared time meets the lower window bound, T meets the upper bound, and all three A2 comparisons can hold. |
   549	
   550	These are arithmetic witnesses to what the verifier comparisons establish, not executed receipt vectors or a claim that an honest issuer necessarily ships them. The issuer's stop duty does not create an additional verifier comparison.
   551	
   552	**Next amendment finding:** narrow the guarantee's heading and any literal “allowed to sign during” description to the actual comparisons, or explicitly change the registered checks. Preserve the inclusive endpoints and chain-time limitation. Do not let the plain dictionary's “window” definition decide this policy question.
   553	
   554	### A03 — the history-depth exclusion needs an explicit counting convention
   555	
   556	A9 49–50 excludes “Bitcoin history deeper than k”; C5 says “Reorganization deeper than k is a residual.” A9 223 sets k = 6. But A9 105 imports “k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1`”, and the A2.1 convention counts the inclusion block as confirmation one. At six confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block.
   557	
   558	If a reorganization's depth means the number of old tip blocks removed, replacing six blocks at that point can remove the anchor. “Deeper than six” excludes seven and above, leaving the equality case apparently covered. If the source uses another depth convention, it must state it. The model's `DepthK` guard is not itself a definition of the ordinary phrase “history deeper than k”.
   559	
   560	**Next amendment finding:** define reorganization depth and state the exact excluded equality boundary relative to confirmation count. Check the intended assumption against the model convention. This is a counting ambiguity with a concrete boundary case, not a claim that a model run failed. It is distinct from the correctly translated C4 six-block gap.
   561	
   562	### A04 — authority capture needs a stated property scope
   563	
   564	A9 40–43 promises that verdicts hold against anyone who “captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)”. A8.3 72–76 defines credential disclosure with honest signing keys secret, but bounds its tested property to preventing reattribution of honest frames, alteration of honest signer sets, and carrying honest signatures onto other bytes or keys. A8.5 123–124 explicitly puts “preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new attestations” out of scope.
   565	
   566	A9's opening can read as protecting the *presentation-to-the-service claim for arbitrary new receipts* under credential capture. That is stronger than protecting an already signed receipt against reattribution. “Signing capability” also needs an object: capability for the original service keys, or for any keys a captured authority can name? Whole-author-sphere capture is excluded, but compromising authority credentials does not necessarily compromise the author's entire operational sphere.
   567	
   568	**Next amendment finding:** state whether the capture claim concerns existing honest receipts, new receipts, or both, and which credentials and signing keys it quantifies over. If new receipts are included, name the invariant that prevents attacker-selected key assertions from establishing service presentation. This is a scope ambiguity, not a demonstrated forged-receipt result. The pilot's short account-control bullet hides it rather than resolving it.
   569	
   570	### A05 — “verified” selection and unpinned evidence need an order of evaluation
   571	
   572	C5 selects “the verified attestation at the lowest block height in the presented OTS proof”. C6 says “A selected anchor (C5) beyond the newest pin is `UNVERIFIABLE`”, “Every supplied header must be pinned”, and “An unselected attestation without its headers supplies no fact and is not a failure”.
   573	
   574	The word “verified” can mean verification including pin coverage, or verification of the proof operations before that coverage check. The first reading appears to prevent an uncovered anchor from becoming selected at all; the second allows selection followed by CANNOT VERIFY. Likewise, a bare lower-height attestation without headers cannot simply win selection by announcing a low height, but the source does not make that candidate rule explicit in these clauses.
   575	
   576	**Next amendment finding:** state the selection stages: which candidates can supply a verified height, when coverage is checked, what makes an anchor selected, and when a headerless candidate is ignored. Include a covered path plus a lower purported/headerless path, and an anchor beyond the pins. This identifies ambiguity in A9's abbreviated rule; it does not assert that the implementation specification lacks an algorithm.
   577	
   578	### A06 — “owner name D” can identify the wrong DNS name
   579	
   580	C4 says “DNSSEC (owner name D …)”; A9 68 identifies D as `wamason.com`. The encoding A9 adopts (A9 149) instead uses `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` as the required TXT owner. `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` 634–635 requires exact matching of that prefixed owner. A9 414–415 rejects a substituted per-key owner name.
   581	
   582	In ordinary DNS language, the owner name is the complete name owning the record. A literal rendering of “owner name D” would therefore check `wamason.com`, not the required prefixed name. “For domain D” can be harmless shorthand for the domain's authority record, but it is not the same as specifying the owner name.
   583	
   584	**Next amendment finding:** say “the fixed authority-record owner for D” and name the exact construction, or explicitly state the intended name. F13's proposed text avoids choosing a different owner and refers to the required name.
   585	
   586	### Apparent source issues that are already resolved, or belong elsewhere
   587	
   588	- **C2's existential presentation:** A9 is explicit. “At some time” preserves the existential quantifier. The pilot loses the issuance event, not the quantifier. No amendment finding about uniqueness is justified.
   589	- **Public publication:** C4's non-claim and A9 495–500's public-publication duty are compatible: the issuer must publish, but the verifier does not prove publication. The pilot must not remove that distinction.
   590	- **Current key safety versus old receipts:** A9 433–440 expressly separates survival after later compromise from unevaluated historical authenticity assumptions. It does not establish past safety merely because the signatures verify.
   591	- **C7's achievement status:** “4 Dec criterion, not yet established” is explicit. The pilot's universal instruction is a translation defect; A9 does not claim a completed general usability result.
   592	- **Six-block git gap:** `g + 5 < r` means at least six height increments. This is not a source contradiction.
   593	- **Stale PROPOSED header:** the signing-act rule and history resolve adoption for this review. Record it as a provenance presentation issue, not evidence that A9 is unsigned.
   594	- **Google custody, receipt ID, and the meaning of INVALID:** the dictionary defects below are not contradictions in A9. A9 explicitly permits custody alternatives and a classical fallback.
   595	
   596	## Style review
   597	
   598	### Length, paragraphs and one idea
   599	
   600	**Sentence length: PASS.** All 79 units fit the applicable limits: no more than 17 words under the stated counting method, including the “Not claimed:” labels when present. The two explicit review instructions S77 and S79 fit 20 words, as do the “must” sentences. No length violation is hidden by a wrapped line. Metadata, fragments, references and notes were also checked.
   601	
   602	**Paragraphs: revise C4.** Pilot 69–73 is one Markdown paragraph. It has eight period-terminated units after the heading label, including three non-claim fragments. If fragments count as sentences, it exceeds six. Counting only full grammatical sentences gives five; Style does not specify how fragments count. Split the claim, checks and non-claims into separate paragraphs or lists so neither reading exceeds six. C5 has six such units; the other paragraphs fit. Heading labels are not counted as sentences.
   603	
   604	**One idea: revise where lists carry the distinction.** S21 combines replay/substitution and backdating; S35 combines several unrelated security failures. The proposed separate bullets preserve their distinct meanings. C1 and C7 also benefit from separate sentences for each check or report field. Parallel wording alone is not a substitute for preserving separate rules.
   605	
   606	### Active voice and clear actors
   607	
   608	| Pilot location | Wording | Assessment and proposed form |
   609	|---|---|---|
   610	| 43 / S29 | “if a key was revoked” | Passive with no actor. Use “V1 does not check key revocation.” [D] This preserves *not evaluated*, rather than promising that every revocation has an identifiable actor in the receipt. |
   611	| 64–65 / S47 | “If a signature is missing, removed or replaced” | “Removed” and “replaced” have an omitted actor. Put conditions in a list: “A required signature is missing”; “An attacker removes a required signature”; “An attacker replaces a required signature.” [D] Keep the required service-signature scope. |
   612	| 59; 62; 66; 72–73; 77; 81 | Repeated “Not claimed:” fragments | Reduced passive wording with no actor. Use “V1 does not establish …” or “V1 does not promise …”, with the actual non-claim as the object. For public publication: “The verifier does not establish that the git commit was public.” [D] |
   613	| 21 / S15 | “The receipt also checks the window” | Grammatically active but the wrong actor; use F03. |
   614	| 70 / S52–S53 | “DNSSEC signs”; “the repository key signs” the repository | Active grammar does not make the actor/object technically correct; use F13 and F14. |
   615	| 5 / S05 | “A faithfulness review must occur” | Active, but does not name the reviewer. Clearer: “A reviewer must compare this text with Amendment 9 before anyone relies on it.” |
   616	
   617	“The receipt is INVALID,” “the protections are true,” “the content is true,” “keys are in different technical systems,” and “the time is not exact” are stative constructions, not passive voice. Do not fail them merely for containing “is,” “are,” or “was.”
   618	
   619	### Dictionary-only project vocabulary: FAIL
   620	
   621	Style 14 limits **project ideas** to the dictionary. It does not restrict ordinary English grammar to a 19-entry word list. The following project vocabulary lacks an entry, uses a forbidden synonym, or needs a narrower definition. Technical proper names are allowed by rule 6, subject to first-use explanation; that exception does not approve every generic security term.
   622	
   623	| Pilot occurrence | Problem |
   624	|---|---|
   625	| 18, heading C6 | “without Tessera” uses “Tessera” alone where the dictionary requires “service”. The introductory proper name “Tessera V1” at 9 is appropriate. |
   626	| 57, 64, 75 | “signed parts” has no dictionary meaning for a frame. It must distinguish the content, each frame, both signatures, and the anchor encoding. |
   627	| 58 | “receipt ID” is approved vocabulary, but the pilot uses it for C1's different property. Its definition's “signed core” is undefined. |
   628	| 31, 48–51, 66–71 | Account-control, repository-control, repository key, git record and git repository concepts lack entries. “Key” is defined only as a private service key, not a repository or DNS authority key. |
   629	| 33, 35, 44, 85 | Signature algorithm, cryptography, key rules, historical safety and revocation/revoked lack entries. These are substantive conditions, not interchangeable ordinary adjectives. |
   630	| 62 | Transaction and replay suitability lack entries. “Second use” describes replay but does not define its limits. |
   631	| 75–78, 81–82 | Bitcoin block, block number, confirmation, proof and pin coverage need defined meanings. “Attestation” at 76 is also a forbidden receipt synonym under a literal reading of the dictionary. In context it means an OTS object, so replacing it with “receipt” is wrong. |
   632	| 81 | Holder is an undefined custody role. Use “the person who keeps the receipt,” or approve the role. |
   633	| 84–86 | Verdict type, historical safety, relying-party test and “bad receipt” are not defined. The last phrase also confuses an invalid receipt with unsafe reliance on a valid one. |
   634	| 94–96 | The editorial notes use existential, pinned-header, missing-capture negative control, replay and reattribute terminology without dictionary entries. Keeping them internal does not make the entire pilot dictionary-only. |
   635	
   636	The absence of “should”, “may” and “shall” does not cure these vocabulary failures.
   637	
   638	**Proposed dictionary work, confined to this review:**
   639	
   640	- Add **frame**: the exact bytes a particular service key signs. Explain that it contains content and common metadata, with a slot-specific fingerprint.
   641	- Add **common metadata**, **key fingerprint**, **slot**, **A_sig** and **receipt anchor**. Keep “content” for customer bytes. Do not use “signed part” as an undefined substitute.
   642	- Repair **receipt ID**: if retained, name the exact `SHA256(A_sig)` object and distinguish it from injective parsing of `A_sig`. A9's adopted encoding defines that anchor digest; neither “signed core” nor content alone is an adequate definition.
   643	- Repair **anchor time**: do not say the *whole receipt* existed at T. The dictionary defines receipt as all returned files, while C5 bounds the frames and exact signatures. Proof outputs and confirmation evidence are outside `A_sig` (A9 290–296; ENCODING-V1 402–405). Resolve source issue A01 before promising a real-world time bound.
   644	- Repair **window**: describe the actual declared-time lower bound and selected-T upper bound. Do not imply that V1 measures the real signing instant or compares T to both endpoints. A02 remains an amendment finding.
   645	- Add **signing capability**, **non-exportable**, **operational sphere**, **issuance event**, **signature algorithm**, **cryptographic assumption**, **key-use assumption**, **history assumption**, **revocation**, **historical security**, and **status capture**. Use the exact source meanings; possession of key bytes and permission to sign must remain distinct.
   646	- Add role-qualified **repository key**, **DNS root key**, and the release-authentication key if mentioned. The service-only “key” definition cannot serve all those roles unchanged.
   647	- Add **DNS snapshot**, **evidence time**, **DNS owner name**, and the git objects **commit**, **tree**, **blob**, **object ID**. Explain the complete path to an authority record.
   648	- Add **Bitcoin block**, **block number**, **header**, **pin**, **confirmation**, **OpenTimestamps proof**, **proof path**, and a term for its Bitcoin attestation. The last term must not be a synonym for the Tessera receipt.
   649	- Add **standing**, **renewal**, **wrapper** and **degraded verification** before restoring O01. Define standing from the governing standing rules, not as mere key safety or revocation. Likewise, renewal is preservation with later evidence; a wrapper is an outer verification layer; degraded verification waives specified checks. These require their registered definitions before publication.
   650	- Add **registered mapping**, **unsafe accept**, **false reject**, **independence conditions**, **negative control**, **companion verifier**, and a defined term for the person using the verifier. An unsafe accept concerns an unsupported relying decision, including decisions involving otherwise VALID receipts.
   651	- Narrow the definitions of **VALID** and **INVALID** to V1's registered checks. “All checks passed” must not imply evaluated revocation, historical security, standing, submitter identity or content truth. An INVALID result is not evidence that customer content itself is false. A later presented proof can also fail the window check for an otherwise unchanged signed receipt (A9 425–426).
   652	- Make **AWS key** and **Google key** definitions conditional on the chosen branch and dated custody choice. A9 351–374 has an ML-DSA hybrid and a P-256 fallback; A9 375–381 permits a hardware token or another provider's HSM. A9 does not universally require Google custody. The pilot does not currently use “Google key”; this is a dictionary/source mismatch, not a pilot sentence defect.
   653	
   654	These are proposals, not approved vocabulary. Accordingly, replacements marked [D] pass the structural rules but still need dictionary work. Claiming that they already meet the current dictionary would repeat the pilot's central problem: hiding a missing prerequisite.
   655	
   656	### Technical names and first-use explanations: FAIL
   657	
   658	- **wamason.com**, first at 9: identify it as the service's own domain name, not merely a customer label.
   659	- **Bitcoin**, first at 16: explain that it supplies the chain evidence and the selected block's time. “Bitcoin fixes T” does not explain the system or its clock limitation.
   660	- **DNS** and **git**, first at 31: explain their roles as the two authority-record systems before using account/repository compromise in the adversary list.
   661	- **AWS**, first at 48: expand Amazon Web Services and identify the issuing account. The dictionary's separate “AWS key” entry is not an explanation of this account.
   662	- **SHA-256** and **OpenTimestamps**, first at 50: explain the hash function and Bitcoin anchoring proof system. These are allowed unchanged names, not dictionary violations merely because they are proper technical names.
   663	- **DNSSEC**, first at 70: expand Domain Name System Security Extensions and explain that it authenticates signed DNS records. “DNSSEC signs” supplies an inaccurate explanation.
   664	
   665	The replacement text also introduces unchanged technical identifiers and git objects; define them before their first use as specified above. A reference to Style does not satisfy “Explain each one when you first use it” inside this pilot.
   666	
   667	### Modals, lists, references and idioms
   668	
   669	**Forbidden modals: PASS.** Case-insensitive whole-word inspection finds no “should”, “may” or “shall” in the pilot. Its uses of “must” and “can” are compatible with Style's modal convention, except that S71 invents the wrong requirement; that is a faithfulness error, not a forbidden-modal error.
   670	
   671	**Steps and conditions: revise.** The four checks and adversary lists already use lists. The condition on the protections at 35, the two window conditions at 21–22, the missing/removed/replaced signature conditions at 64–65, and the pin condition at 81–82 need lists under rule 8. The proposed replacements supply them. Format the restored cryptographic assumptions, time checks and negative-control conditions as lists too.
   672	
   673	**Section-number references: one violation.** Owner note S78 says “In section 2” in the body (Pilot 96); move the locator to References. Heading numbers themselves are not body cross-references. The existing references section is appropriately placed.
   674	
   675	**Idioms/metaphors/humour:** no humour or idiom finding. “Bitcoin fixes T” is terse but can mean “determines T”; its problem is missing explanation and selection detail, not necessarily a metaphor. “A receipt proves” has the substantive qualification problem in F01. “The receipt checks” names the wrong actor in F03. Do not inflate those into unrelated style findings.
   676	
   677	**Headings:** “What Tessera V1 promises” and “A safe decision” need the same visible qualification as the claims: C7 is a criterion, not an achieved result. The C1 label “The bytes” can become “The content” to use the dictionary's project term. Replace the C6 heading “Without Tessera” with “Without the service.” The claim labels C1–C7 themselves are reference identifiers and can remain.
   678	
   679	## Validation and limits of this review
   680	
   681	The ledger covers all 79 units in original order, including the four boundary fragments, every non-claim fragment and the owner notes. The primary decisions are 55 FAITHFUL, 1 ADDS, 15 DROPS and 8 CHANGES, with five additional omission-only findings. The six A-series findings concern source wording and scope; they are not silently repaired by the pilot. Sentence counts and forbidden-modal checks are mechanical; active voice, vocabulary, and semantic decisions are manual judgments with source quotations.
   682	
   683	This is a document review, not an implementation, formal-discharge or completed relying-party-test result. Proposed wording has no governing effect. Items marked [D] cannot receive a dictionary-compliance pass until the style vocabulary is repaired, and items marked [A] retain the stated source rule pending an amendment decision.
     1	# Tessera plain-language style (inspired by ASD-STE100)
     2	
     3	Owner instance, 2026-10-16. The author asked for documentation "written so someone whose native language isn't
     4	Claude can understand it". These rules adapt the main writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English).
     5	We do not claim STE conformance: we have no approved STE dictionary check. We use the structure rules and our own
     6	dictionary.
     7	
     8	Revised 2026-10-16 after the faithfulness review of the A9 pilot (`docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md`,
     9	"Proposed dictionary work"). The revision adds the entries that the review lists and repairs receipt, anchor time,
    10	window, authority record, key, AWS key, VALID and INVALID. It replaces "Google key" with "second key". It removes
    11	"receipt ID", because its object was not defined. These entries are owner proposals. A reviewer must check each
    12	new entry against its registered source before publication.
    13	
    14	## Rules
    15	
    16	1. One sentence gives one idea.
    17	2. A sentence has a maximum of 20 words if it tells the reader to do something, and 25 words if it describes something.
    18	3. A paragraph has a maximum of 6 sentences and one topic. A fragment such as a list item counts as a sentence.
    19	4. Use the active voice. Say who or what does the action.
    20	5. Use only the words in the dictionary below for project ideas. Each word has one meaning. Do not use a synonym.
    21	6. Use technical names (for example "DNSSEC", "Bitcoin", "SHA-256") without change. Explain each one when you first use it.
    22	7. Do not use idioms, metaphors or humour.
    23	8. Write steps and conditions as numbered or bulleted lists.
    24	9. Write "must" for a rule and "can" for a possibility. Do not use "should", "may" or "shall".
    25	10. Do not refer to a document section number in the body. Put references at the end.
    26	
    27	## Faithfulness
    28	
    29	A plain document does not replace the signed or governing text. If the two differ, the governing text applies, and
    30	the difference is a finding. Before publication, a reviewer checks each plain document against its source. The
    31	reviewer confirms three things: the plain document contains no new rule, it removes no rule, and it changes no
    32	meaning. The reviewer records each place where the source was unclear or contradictory.
    33	
    34	A plain document does not resolve an unclear source. It renders the source as written and adds a visible marker,
    35	for example "[A9 is unclear here: see A10 draft, item A01.]". The marker names the item that must resolve it.
    36	
    37	## Dictionary
    38	
    39	Code-style names (for example `A_sig`, `NOT_EVALUATED`) are technical identifiers. Use them without change and
    40	explain them at first use.
    41	
    42	### Content, receipts and the service
    43	
