   117	## Findings for non-FAITHFUL sentences
   118	
   119	### F01
   120	
   121	**S07 — DROPS the condition on the four checks.** Pilot: “A receipt proves four things:”. A9 32–34: “Provided the service's signing capability was not misused within the key's window, a V1 receipt lets anyone, with no network and no help from Tessera, check four things”. The pilot's later statement about cryptography does not restore the condition on misuse. “Proves” also conceals the distinction between a conditional claim and an unconditional fact.
   122	
   123	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   124	
   125	```text
   126	A receipt supports these four checks under both conditions:
   127	- The registered cryptographic and key-use assumptions hold.
   128	- Nobody misused the service's signing capability within the window.
   129	```
   130	
   131	### F02
   132	
   133	**S10 and S51 — DROPS the evidence-time qualification.** Pilot: “Two authority records said that these keys belong to wamason.com, for a stated window.” / “Two authority records said that the keys belong to wamason.com, for the window.” C4: “authenticated K for D, with the issuance window, at their evidence times.” These are historical assertions evaluated at particular evidence times, not a claim of continuing authority. The later descriptions never restore those evaluation times.
   134	
   135	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   136	
   137	```text
   138	Both authority records say the two keys belong to wamason.com.
   139	They give the window in which those keys can issue receipts.
   140	The verifier must check each authority record at that record's evidence time.
   141	```
   142	
   143	F13 and F14 specify the different checks for the two records.
   144	
   145	### F03
   146	
   147	**S15 — CHANGES who checks the window.** Pilot: “The receipt also checks the window.” A9 419–421: “Verifier rule (C4). A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless” the two stated comparisons hold. A receipt supplies evidence; the verifier performs the check.
   148	
   149	Proposed plain text:
   150	
   151	```text
   152	The verifier also checks the window.
   153	```
   154	
   155	Keep S16 and S17 as a bulleted list after that sentence. Their two inclusive comparisons are faithful. Do not replace them with “both times must be inside the window”: A9 does not require that.
   156	
   157	### F04
   158	
   159	**S21 — DROPS ordinary replay and reattribution.** Pilot: “The attacker uses a true receipt again for different content, or puts a false date on it.” A9 40: “replays, reattributes or backdates receipts”. Reuse with different content describes one substitution attempt. It does not cover another presentation of the same receipt and content, or a false claim about which keys or service made it. C2 explicitly leaves transaction suitability unresolved.
   160	
   161	Proposed plain text **[D]** (three bullets in the attack list):
   162	
   163	```text
   164	- The attacker presents a receipt again.
   165	- The attacker claims that different keys or a different service made the receipt.
   166	- The attacker claims an earlier issue time for a receipt.
   167	```
   168	
   169	These protections do not promise transaction-specific replay prevention. Keep C2's transaction non-claim beside them.
   170	
   171	### F05
   172	
   173	**S23 — DROPS later signing capability without key theft.** Pilot: “The attacker steals a key after Bitcoin anchored the receipt.” A9 42–43: “steals a key, gains signing capability, or breaks a signature algorithm after a receipt was anchored.” S24 covers the algorithm case. A non-exportable key can remain secret while an attacker gains permission to use it (C3; A9 387–388).
   174	
   175	Proposed additional bullet **[D]**:
   176	
   177	```text
   178	- The attacker gains signing capability after Bitcoin anchors the receipt.
   179	```
   180	
   181	Keep the theft and algorithm bullets. Do not equate signing capability with possession of private key bytes.
   182	
   183	### F06
   184	
   185	**S25 — DROPS the registered nature and specificity of the assumptions.** Pilot: “These protections are true only if the cryptography, the key rules and Bitcoin work as expected.” A9 43: “under the registered cryptographic, key-use and chain assumptions.” “Work as expected” substitutes an unspecified expectation for a registered condition. It can also imply that correct cryptographic calculations alone establish historical key safety, which C7 explicitly does not evaluate.
   186	
   187	Proposed plain text **[D]**:
   188	
   189	```text
   190	These protections depend on the registered cryptographic, key-use and Bitcoin history assumptions.

