   527	## Findings about A9 itself for the next amendment
   528	
   529	These are source findings exposed by the rendering, separate from translation defects and dictionary defects. Some are ambiguities requiring clarification, not demonstrated failures of the implementation or formal models. The pilot must not settle them silently. References outside the requested paragraphs identify rules A9 itself invokes; they do not substitute another draft for A9.
   530	
   531	### A01 — “existed no later than T” needs an explicit clock meaning
   532	
   533	A9 36–37: “the bytes and both signatures existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin.” C5 repeats “existed no later than T” and also says “Header times are chain-time proxies.” A9 444–446 says a finite UTC cutoff needs an additional backward-skew assumption and “A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C”.
   534	
   535	Those sentences do not support the ordinary-language interpretation that the bytes existed before the real-world instant denoted by T. An inclusion block can carry a backward-skewed header time. The preimage must exist before inclusion; it need not exist before that header's nominal UTC reading. Merely saying “not exact” or “close” does not solve this.
   536	
   537	**Next amendment finding:** explicitly distinguish existence before inclusion in the selected block from an upper bound in real-world time. State which meaning the claim adopts. If it adopts a UTC bound, register the additional assumption and evidence; otherwise qualify the opening and C5. This is a source-level tension, not only the pilot's unsupported closeness assertion. S11 and S59 reproduce the source faithfully but inherit it.
   538	
   539	### A02 — “inside the window” overstates the actual two comparisons
   540	
   541	A9 441: “Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.” A9 419–421 specifies only declared issue time ≥ not-before and T ≤ not-after, and explicitly says “T itself is not compared with not-before.” A9's opening and A9.1 repeat those asymmetric comparisons. A9 407 calls the values an “Issuance window”.
   542	
   543	These are not a rule that both time values, or the actual act of signing, lie inside the interval. Two numerical examples expose the distinction under A2's preserved tolerances:
   544	
   545	| Example | Declared issue time | T | Designated sixth-block time | Why the stated comparisons permit it |
   546	|---|---|---|---|---|
   547	| Before-start T | window start | start minus 1 hour | start plus 1 hour | T ≥ declared minus 24 hours; all upper time bounds hold. T need not reach the window start. |
   548	| After-end declared time | window end plus 1 hour | window end | end plus 1 hour | Declared time meets the lower window bound, T meets the upper bound, and all three A2 comparisons can hold. |
   549	
   550	These are arithmetic witnesses to what the verifier comparisons establish, not executed receipt vectors or a claim that an honest issuer necessarily ships them. The issuer's stop duty does not create an additional verifier comparison.
   551	
   552	**Next amendment finding:** narrow the guarantee's heading and any literal “allowed to sign during” description to the actual comparisons, or explicitly change the registered checks. Preserve the inclusive endpoints and chain-time limitation. Do not let the plain dictionary's “window” definition decide this policy question.
   553	
   554	### A03 — the history-depth exclusion needs an explicit counting convention
   555	
   556	A9 49–50 excludes “Bitcoin history deeper than k”; C5 says “Reorganization deeper than k is a residual.” A9 223 sets k = 6. But A9 105 imports “k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1`”, and the A2.1 convention counts the inclusion block as confirmation one. At six confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block.
   557	
   558	If a reorganization's depth means the number of old tip blocks removed, replacing six blocks at that point can remove the anchor. “Deeper than six” excludes seven and above, leaving the equality case apparently covered. If the source uses another depth convention, it must state it. The model's `DepthK` guard is not itself a definition of the ordinary phrase “history deeper than k”.
   559	
   560	**Next amendment finding:** define reorganization depth and state the exact excluded equality boundary relative to confirmation count. Check the intended assumption against the model convention. This is a counting ambiguity with a concrete boundary case, not a claim that a model run failed. It is distinct from the correctly translated C4 six-block gap.
   561	
   562	### A04 — authority capture needs a stated property scope
   563	
   564	A9 40–43 promises that verdicts hold against anyone who “captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)”. A8.3 72–76 defines credential disclosure with honest signing keys secret, but bounds its tested property to preventing reattribution of honest frames, alteration of honest signer sets, and carrying honest signatures onto other bytes or keys. A8.5 123–124 explicitly puts “preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new attestations” out of scope.
   565	
   566	A9's opening can read as protecting the *presentation-to-the-service claim for arbitrary new receipts* under credential capture. That is stronger than protecting an already signed receipt against reattribution. “Signing capability” also needs an object: capability for the original service keys, or for any keys a captured authority can name? Whole-author-sphere capture is excluded, but compromising authority credentials does not necessarily compromise the author's entire operational sphere.
   567	
   568	**Next amendment finding:** state whether the capture claim concerns existing honest receipts, new receipts, or both, and which credentials and signing keys it quantifies over. If new receipts are included, name the invariant that prevents attacker-selected key assertions from establishing service presentation. This is a scope ambiguity, not a demonstrated forged-receipt result. The pilot's short account-control bullet hides it rather than resolving it.
   569	
   570	### A05 — “verified” selection and unpinned evidence need an order of evaluation
   571	
   572	C5 selects “the verified attestation at the lowest block height in the presented OTS proof”. C6 says “A selected anchor (C5) beyond the newest pin is `UNVERIFIABLE`”, “Every supplied header must be pinned”, and “An unselected attestation without its headers supplies no fact and is not a failure”.
   573	
   574	The word “verified” can mean verification including pin coverage, or verification of the proof operations before that coverage check. The first reading appears to prevent an uncovered anchor from becoming selected at all; the second allows selection followed by CANNOT VERIFY. Likewise, a bare lower-height attestation without headers cannot simply win selection by announcing a low height, but the source does not make that candidate rule explicit in these clauses.
   575	
   576	**Next amendment finding:** state the selection stages: which candidates can supply a verified height, when coverage is checked, what makes an anchor selected, and when a headerless candidate is ignored. Include a covered path plus a lower purported/headerless path, and an anchor beyond the pins. This identifies ambiguity in A9's abbreviated rule; it does not assert that the implementation specification lacks an algorithm.
   577	
   578	### A06 — “owner name D” can identify the wrong DNS name
   579	
   580	C4 says “DNSSEC (owner name D …)”; A9 68 identifies D as `wamason.com`. The encoding A9 adopts (A9 149) instead uses `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` as the required TXT owner. `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` 634–635 requires exact matching of that prefixed owner. A9 414–415 rejects a substituted per-key owner name.
   581	
   582	In ordinary DNS language, the owner name is the complete name owning the record. A literal rendering of “owner name D” would therefore check `wamason.com`, not the required prefixed name. “For domain D” can be harmless shorthand for the domain's authority record, but it is not the same as specifying the owner name.
   583	
   584	**Next amendment finding:** say “the fixed authority-record owner for D” and name the exact construction, or explicitly state the intended name. F13's proposed text avoids choosing a different owner and refers to the required name.
   585	
