   388	
   389	- **Authority: independent release allowlisting** (A9.5 "Trust-configuration authority", OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14).
   390	  The trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials.
   391	  Each verifier release compiles in the SHA-256 of exactly one trust configuration; the config file itself carries no
   392	  signature. Releases are signed by the **release key**, which neither the DNS credential, the repository key nor the
   393	  issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key and its access boundary. Capture of any
   394	  authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release, authorize a configuration hash, or replace the
   395	  compiled trust root or policy catalog. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise of the release key is
   396	  distribution compromise, a stated boundary (A9 "The boundaries"). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G08]
   397	  - A config whose SHA-256 is not the compiled hash, or that fails the section 15.5 schema, makes the verifier refuse
   398	    to run with exit error `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`: no verdict.
   399	  - Bootstrap: the release public key is published with the verifier's documentation and relying-party text, and its
   400	    fingerprint is in the freeze record. A new release key is introduced only by a release signed by the current one.
   401	  - `version` starts at `"1"` and increases by 1; `previous_sha256` is 64 zeros for version 1, else the prior config's
   402	    hash. Every report carries the config version and hash as diagnostics; reproducing a verdict exactly (A9.4) uses
   403	    the release that compiled that hash.
   404	  - Required test: a captured repository signer (and, separately, a captured DNS credential and the issuing AWS
   405	    account) signs a config adding its own KSK, repository key and pins; the verifier refuses it.
   406	  - **OPEN:** release-key custody, holder and access boundary; the release artefact and release-signature format
   407	    (section 14).
   408	  - *Dated correction 2026-10-14:* the 2026-10-13 resolutions draft proposed a dedicated trust-config key giving the
   409	    config a detached rule-O signature, with append-only enforced in CI. Withdrawn: key inequality does not establish
   410	    independence, and CI cannot stop an attacker who bypasses it (`CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md` F04).
   411	
   412	- **Root KSKs, three periods each.** The trust configuration records each KSK's signing and reliance periods
   413	  separately from its publication and revocation dates. [A9:153; A9:302-303] The instrument spike found that
   414	  conflating signing with publication falsely rejected every snapshot from 2026-10-11. [SR:73-78]
   415	- **Root rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time if some trusted KSK whose **reliance
   416	  period** contains that time validates it. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance lifetime L; the reliance
   417	  interval is [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is an acceptance-policy value from cited RRSIG validity evidence,
   418	  labelled observed, not a proven global maximum. The extension admits authentic pre-retirement evidence anchored
   419	  later and does not prove signing before retirement: a thief of a retired KSK can manufacture eligible evidence
   420	  during it (A9.5; AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-13, kind CORRECT). *Dated correction 2026-10-13: the earlier wording
   421	  ("maximum validity … it made"; "usable for that long and no longer") overclaimed provenance and is withdrawn.* The rule is existential and
   422	  order-independent: a candidate that fails is informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible
   423	  key has been tried. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure.
   424	  [A9.5, A9:296-303, OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13 after `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F2]
