   477	## A1.6 Temporal anchor semantics, stated precisely
   478	
   479	The original's forgery argument ("producing a valid OTS anchor at the
   480	original time") compressed what the anchor actually establishes. Precisely:
   481	
   482	- **Vocabulary.** `declared_issue_time` — the issue time the issuer claims,
   483	  committed inside the signed canonical bytes. `anchor_time` — the Bitcoin
   484	  block time of the OTS anchor over those bytes.
   485	- **The anchor is an upper bound on creation time.** The anchor proves the
   486	  bytes existed *not after* `anchor_time` — nothing more. It provides **no
   487	  lower bound**: old bytes can be stamped today, and a forger with
   488	  compromised keys can sign new bytes today. What the anchor prevents is
   489	  *backdating* — obtaining a conforming anchor in the past for bytes
   490	  created now. New forgeries dated honestly-now are the key-compromise /
   491	  misissuance classes (§3.1), met operationally, not by the anchor.
   492	- **The verifier enforces a two-sided consistency check (P5).**
   493	  `declared_issue_time − ε ≤ anchor_time ≤ declared_issue_time + δ`, with
   494	  strict defaults δ = 72 hours and ε = 24 hours, both declared per receipt
   495	  and ratified (or revised, on the record) at Band 0 exit. Temporal
   496	  consistency is non-waivable (A1.2.1). The issuance protocol treats
   497	  anchor confirmation — at minimum depth k, strict default k = 6 (P5
   498	  corollary) — within δ as part of successful issuance; late or reorged
   499	  anchors force re-issue.
   500	- **Layer 2 assumptions, extended.** To the original's cited assumptions
   501	  (signature unforgeability, SHA-256 resistance, OTS/Bitcoin timestamp
   502	  assumptions) add, explicitly: Bitcoin block timestamps are inexact —
   503	  consensus rules (median-time-past, the two-hour future bound) bound but
   504	  do not eliminate skew — absorbed into δ and ε; anchors at depth ≥ k are
   505	  treated as permanent (a reorganization deeper than k is out of model,
   506	  subsumed by the history assumption that follows); and **the
   507	  proof-of-work history remains intact and available over the
   508	  adjudication horizon**. A
   509	  fifty-year adjudication story rests on Bitcoin's fifty-year survival;
   510	  stating that plainly is the declare-what-you-cannot-see ethos, applied
   511	  to the anchor itself.
   512	
