   285	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   286	
   287	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   288	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   289	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   290	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   291	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   292	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   293	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   294	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   295	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   296	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   297	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   298	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   299	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
   300	  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
   301	  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
   302	  The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member `object_format` as the string
   303	  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
   304	  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
   305	  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
   306	  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
   307	  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
   308	  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
   309	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   310	  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
   311	  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
   312	  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
   313	  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
   314	  RRSIG's inception/expiration checks at the snapshot's own verified anchored time. This extension permits authentic
   315	  pre-retirement evidence anchored later; it does not establish that signing occurred before retirement. A thief of
   316	  a retired KSK can manufacture eligible root evidence during the extension. Exposure is bounded by the reliance
   317	  endpoint in snapshot chain time, not by a finite UTC duration under A2.1. Historical authority-key safety and the
   318	  continuing anchor/history assumptions remain necessary and are not evaluated by V1. **AUTHOR ADOPTED, 2026-10-13**
   319	  (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2; kind CORRECT: the withdrawn cutoff claimed a protection DNSSEC itself does not
   320	  supply, since resolvers accept a retired KSK's signatures until they expire). The author: *"I'd argue that the
   321	  'narrowing of protection' wasn't a real narrowing, it was us admitting the real level of protection given the
   322	  constraints of the project."* The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
   323	  informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
   324	  is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
   325	  reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
   326	- **Trust-configuration authority** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, after `docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/` F04). The
   327	  trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials. Each
   328	  verifier release compiles in the hash of its configuration. Releases are authenticated by a key that neither the
   329	  DNS credential, the repository key nor the issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key
   330	  and its access boundary. Capture of an authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release,
   331	  authorize a configuration hash, or replace the compiled trust root. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise
   332	  of the release key is distribution compromise, a stated boundary.
   333	- **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). Trust configuration entries are append-only: KSKs, header
   334	  pins and repository keys are never removed, and finalized period endpoints never change. Repository trust entries
   335	  name D, the key and an inclusive signing interval; the git assertion passes this trust check if some configured
   336	  entry for D verifies gpgsig and its interval contains the signed commit's own verified anchored time. Repository
   337	  entries have no KSK-style reliance extension. Unknown material cannot establish this required check. Append-only
   338	  extensions preserve previously usable trust witnesses; newly covered evidence can improve an earlier UNVERIFIABLE
   339	  result. Exact stable-field identity remains the contract for identical declared inputs. Closing an open period must
   340	  not retrospectively exclude evidence previously accepted under that entry; the pre-freeze configuration records the
   341	  endpoint/update convention explicitly. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   342	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   343	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   344	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
   345	
   346	## A9.6 Signers, the issuance window, and what they guarantee — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12
   347	
   348	**Signer profile, conditional.** The hybrid records the author's stated preference (2026-10-12: *"I think we
   349	should build our V1 with a PQ implementation"*). It is not a claim requirement: no sentence of C1–C7 needs it. It
   350	is the first item cut if 30 Oct slips (A9.2).
     1	# Tessera guarantee tiers — draft
     2	
     3	> **STATUS: DRAFT, 2026-10-07, owner instance (Claude Opus 5.5), from the author's suggestion that day:**
     4	> *"identify what someone just trying to stand up a test attestation service could do (minimal guarantees)
     5	> and then identify what each additional layer allows us to claim."* Not adopted. Nothing here changes a
     6	> registered sentence. Tier contents are a first cut, to be argued with.
     7	
     8	## The idea
     9	
    10	Each tier is a deployment choice plus **the one additional claim it buys**, and the evidence for that claim.
    11	A deployer picks a tier, and a receipt states its tier, so a relying party knows exactly what it is being
    12	told. Every tier keeps the defining constraint: **a receipt verifies with the Tessera service gone**, from the
    13	bundle plus an archived trust-anchor store. A tier that needs a live service does not exist.
    14	
    15	| Tier | The deployer adds | The claim it buys | Main new attack it must answer | Evidence today |
    16	|---|---|---|---|---|
    17	| **T0: anchored bytes** | one issuer key; canonical frame; an OpenTimestamps anchor | *These framed bytes existed no later than the anchor time and were signed by whoever held key K.* Nothing about who K is. | backdating; byte substitution; type confusion | P1, P5, P7 checked; **P8 open** |
    18	| **T1: one authority channel** | K published under one domain via DNSSEC (or a git repo, which counts only with its own OTS anchor) | *K was published by whoever controlled domain D at issuance.* | issuer↔domain not tied (G-21); a stolen channel key; stale-status replay within an RRSIG window | P3, P10 single chain; G-21 missing |
    19	| **T2: redundant channels** | a second, independent channel (DNS + repo); required channels fixed by **verifier policy** | *The claim survives compromise of any one channel* (strict mode). | a bundle choosing its own required set (G-17); one channel leaked | **the capstone**: strict cases (a)–(c), P2, P10 |
    20	| **T3: diverse jurisdictions** | *n* keys under *n* country-code registries (e.g. .cn, .in, .br, .za); staggered lifetimes, none outliving domain ownership; *m*-of-*n* | *Forging, or hiding a revocation, needs coordinated compromise in at least m jurisdictions.* | coordinated capture; registry coercion | not modelled; generalises the capstone's cases from 2 channels to *n* |
    21	| **T4: long-term validity** | revocation status captured after the anchor (LTV-style, RFC 5126 / ETSI EN 319 102-1); the archived trust-anchor store bound into the bundle; evidence-record renewal (RFC 4998); compromise time via invalidity date (RFC 5280) | *Revocation at or before the anchor is caught, and the receipt still verifies in 2106.* | stale-status replay (freshness bound); hash or signature algorithm ageing | P6 is right as a rule, but **missing a carrier** (G-27); not modelled |
    22	
    23	**Across all tiers: the adjudicator's residual (option A).** A key compromise discovered after the window
    24	(stolen in March, revoked in June) does not change the verifier's verdict (R-5.22). An adjudicator must be told
    25	that such evidence can exist and how to weigh it. The verdict is mechanical; the judgement is not.
    26	
    27	## Authority capture: out of scope to prevent, in scope to make visible (added 2026-10-08)
    28	
    29	The author, 2026-10-08: *"perhaps the right way to attack the problem is distribution and admission that
    30	governance changes are out of scope"*, and *"Authority capture must be visible in such instances. For an
    31	adjudicator to be convinced incorrectly requires the takeover be invisible."*
    32	
    33	**Out of scope.** Stopping a captured or merged authority from issuing new attestations under its own keys.
    34	Jurisdictions can merge; a single authority can emerge. Distribution (T3) makes capture costly and unlikely,
    35	but cannot rule it out.
    36	
    37	**In scope, after capture.** "Capture" here means disclosed authority-channel credentials while honest
    38	signing keys stay secret (the capstone's cases (c) and (d)). Under it, the capstone's case-(c)/(d) companions
    39	test that a captor cannot:
    40	- reattribute an honest party's existing frames;
    41	- alter an honest signer set;
    42	- carry an honest signature onto other bytes or keys.
    43	
    44	Separately, the temporal-anchor models (P5/P6, TLA+), under their own assumptions, are meant to stop anything
    45	being backdated into the time before the capture. The capstone runs do not test that.
    46	
    47	**In scope: making capture visible.** The record already splits this two ways (Band 1 docket items 25–27,
    48	including the author's earlier *"change of control for companies is generally visible"*):
    49	- **Open capture**, such as governance change or legal change of control. *Often* visible, not visible by
    50	  construction. Anchoring prevents backdating, but a governance change need not alter cryptographic
    51	  continuity: new controllers can keep issuing under the same keys. Visibility then rests on public records
    52	  outside Tessera, and on whatever the governance record (T3) captured at issuance.
    53	- **Silent capture**, such as credential theft or an insider. It is invisible by default, because the captor
    54	  issues under the legitimate key.
    55	  - The record has three candidate mechanisms: fork legibility (item 25, the forward link, visible to any
    56	    holder of both successors), third-party log buildability (item 27, *"the design is a floor, not a
    57	    ceiling"*), and bounded key lifetimes (item 26).
    58	  - **No symbolic model leaks the issuer key**, so this case is unmodelled.
    59	
    60	**Governance diversity bounds; it does not guarantee** (the author, 2026-10-08: *"governments change,
    61	boundaries change, rules change. Any adjudicator 80 years hence … just needs to be able to see that. What I
    62	don't think we can do is guarantee it, we can only bound it."*). T3 therefore records the governance
    63	structure **as it was at issuance**, in two parts:
    64	- **Mechanically checkable.** The DNSSEC chain of trust (root → ccTLD → domain) proves which registries each
    65	  channel's evidence ran through. The verifier can state and check: "forgery at issuance required at least m
    66	  of these n channels, through these jurisdictions."
    67	- **Declared.** Who held each registration, under which legal entity, signed and anchored at issuance. This
    68	  is a contemporaneous claim for the adjudicator to weigh, not a verifier input.
    69	
    70	**Diversity is per layer** (author, 2026-10-09, correcting the owner: the author's domains *"do not present
    71	jurisdictional diversity … there is registrar diversity"*). A domain's authority passes through four layers, and
    72	an attacker needs only the weakest. The governance record must state each layer, and "m of n" is meaningful per
    73	layer:
    74	
    75	| Layer | What it controls | Example (the author's five domains, 2026-10-09) |
    76	|---|---|---|
    77	| Registry (TLD operator) | delegation and DS in the parent zone | several TLDs, one holder |
    78	| Registrar (account) | nameservers, DS replacement or removal | diverse: GoDaddy, register.ca, Namecheap |
    79	| DNS operator | zone signing, served keys | single: Cloudflare |
    80	| Holder (person) | all of the above | single |
    81	
    82	T3's claim: *at issuance, compromise required at least m of n channels, through these recorded
    83	jurisdictions, held by these declared parties. Whether they were in fact independent, and what happened to
    84	them since, is the adjudicator's judgement from the record.*
    85	
    86	**The claim this gives, as a target, not a result:** *under the stated capture model, pre-capture honest
    87	material cannot be reattributed, re-membered or re-bound, and backdating is excluded under the anchor's
    88	assumptions. Post-capture misdirection requires the capture to be unnoticed by any surviving holder of the
    89	honest lineage* (A3's survivability floor: "visible failure while an observer possessing the relevant state
    90	survives"). Preserved evidence still has availability and interpretation limits (A3 §A3.4). This draft is
    91	**not** part of the Amendment 8 approval. Its proposed deferral of T3/T4 obligations past Band 0 must be
    92	reconciled with existing commitments before any adoption.
    93	
    94	## The freshness rule, per tier
    95	
    96	- **T1:** status evidence must be no staler than one bound F relative to `anchor_time`. In practice F is the
    97	  channel's RRSIG validity window, which is weak.
    98	- **T3:** at least *m* of *n* channels must be fresh within F. Key lifetimes cap F from above.
    99	- **T4:** the captured status is itself anchored, so its own time is evidenced, not asserted.
   100	
   101	## What this could mean for Band 0 (a proposal; the scope decision is the author's)
   102	
   103	- **Band 0 verifies T0–T2 completely.** That is most of what exists: the capstone is T2's composition. P8 is
   104	  the real gap at T0, and G-17 and G-21 are spec fixes at T1/T2.
   105	- **T3 and T4 are registered as layered claims**, each with its own model obligation (the attacker-plus-time
   106	  revocation model, an *m*-of-*n* generalisation of the capstone's cases), done after Band 0. They are not
   107	  conditions of Band 0's exit.
   108	- **Commercially,** tiers can be offered separately, or all of them implemented to show what is possible.
   109	
   110	## Open questions in this draft
   111	
   112	1. Is T1 with git alone a real tier, or does git only ever count alongside DNS?
   113	2. Where does standing (the terminal lineage record) sit: T2 or T4?
   114	3. Does T0 need any revocation story, or are short-lived keys plus "nothing about who K is" enough?

