   255	  source.
   447	## Source clauses with no pilot sentence
   448	
   449	These are additional DROPS findings. Each quotes the nearest pilot text to show the omission in context. A sentence ledger alone cannot detect a whole missing sentence.
   450	
   451	### O01 — capabilities excluded from V1
   452	
   453	Pilot boundary list: “V1 does not tell you these things:” followed by sender, content meaning, revocation and historical safety. A9 46–47: “It offers no standing, renewal, wrappers or degraded verification”. None of these four exclusions appears elsewhere in the pilot. The lack of lower time bounds and priority *is* restored in C5, so those are not counted as missing.
   454	
   455	Proposed addition **[D]**:
   456	
   457	```text
   458	V1 offers none of these features:
   459	- standing;
   460	- renewal;
   461	- wrappers;
   462	- degraded verification.
   463	```
   464	
   465	The current dictionary has no entries for these concepts; silently omitting them is not a dictionary-compliance strategy.
   466	
   467	### O02 — a non-exportable key can still sign for an attacker
   468	
   469	Pilot C3 ends: “A person who controls the AWS account can make both keys sign.” C3 continues in A9: “the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse.” This matters because the source distinguishes stealing key material from gaining signing capability. The pilot's explanation otherwise invites the wrong inference that hardware protection prevents this attack.
   470	
   471	Proposed addition **[D]**:
   472	
   473	```text
   474	Hardware prevents export of the protected key.
   475	It does not prevent an attacker from using the permitted signing path.
   476	```
   477	
   478	This describes the source's non-exportable key, not a universal property of every kind of hardware.
   479	
   480	### O03 — what authority evidence does not establish
   481	
   482	Pilot C4 non-claims: “Not claimed: revocation checks. Not claimed: that the git record was public. Not claimed: an order in clock time.” C4 also says: “The verifier fixes the required set”; “Neither channel commits to any receipt”; and “DNSSEC evidence establishes no publication before issue.” All three are missing. Historical authority evidence is not authority endorsement of this individual receipt. DNS evidence does not inherit the git evidence's pre-commitment order.
   483	
   484	Proposed additions **[D]**:
   485	
   486	```text
   487	The verifier fixes the required set of authority records.
   488	The receipt cannot choose that set.
   489	Neither authority record commits to an individual receipt.
   490	The DNS evidence does not establish that the DNS record was public before issue.
   491	```
   492	
   493	C4's git public-publication non-claim remains. A9 495–500 separately retains public publication as an issuer duty that the verifier cannot prove.
   494	
   495	### O04 — C6's remaining limits and ignored evidence
   496	
   497	Pilot C6: “The verdict depends only on the receipt, the verifier and its trust file.” Its remaining text covers custody and “If T is after the last Bitcoin block…”. C6 also says: “Dead-project reconstruction not demonstrated” and “An unselected attestation without its headers supplies no fact and is not a failure”. Both are missing. F19 separately restores the all-supplied-header rule.
   498	
   499	Proposed additions **[D, A]**:
   500	
   501	```text
   502	V1 has not demonstrated reconstruction after the project ends.
   503	The verifier must ignore an unselected attestation that supplies no headers.
   504	That attestation establishes nothing and does not cause a failed check.
   505	```
   506	
   507	Here “attestation” is specifically an OpenTimestamps proof object, not a Tessera receipt. It needs distinct vocabulary; applying the dictionary's receipt synonym mechanically would change the object. A05 addresses the selection ambiguity in A9.
   508	
   509	### O05 — C7's assigned build evidence
   510	
   511	Pilot C7 mentions only: “The relying-party test on 4 December 2026 checks this. That test has not occurred yet.” C7 also says: “Assigned build evidence includes A9.4's missing-capture negative control.” A9 282–283 specifies: “for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.” That is separate build evidence, not the human comprehension test. No pilot sentence states it.
   512	
   513	Proposed addition **[D]**:
   514	
   515	```text
   516	The build test must include a negative control.
   517	The control uses a receipt with these properties:
   518	- All V1 checks can run and pass.
   519	- Only status capture is missing.
   520	A companion verifier must fail the test under either condition:
   521	- It gives INVALID only because status capture is missing.
   522	- It gives CANNOT VERIFY only because status capture is missing.
   523	```
   524	
   525	Define status capture as the historical status evidence that V1 does not require, and define companion verifier and negative control. This does not allow a missing signature, required authority record or pinned header to pass.
   526	
   527	## Findings about A9 itself for the next amendment

